    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d94">

                <pb facs="0086" n="74" xml:id="pg74"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">851G.00/12–1946: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Batavia</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Foote</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Batavia</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-12-19T17:00:00+07:30">December 19, 1946—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 20—12:10 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>486. For Vincent from Moffat: “If Deptel 436, December 16, to Singapore<anchor
                        n="80" xml:id="d94-f1"/> sent before receipt my long telegram from
                        Saigon,<anchor n="81" xml:id="d94-f2"/> Dept, in light views expressed, may
                    prefer my return Washington as planned instead of proceeding Canberra. For
                    reasons stated feel settlement Vietnam question extremely difficult and
                    conditions dangerous with possibility deliberate Vietnam spread of conflict to
                    Cambodia and Laos. Believe possibility US assistance should be earnestly
                    considered despite risk of rebuff or unpopularity, especially in view apparent
                    French confidence US resulting from Siamese settlement.”</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Foote</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
